## Reviewer Report

**Review-session bound (disclosed per "No silent caps"):** This review had no tool execution available (Bash/Read disabled in this session — confirmed by the framing note preceding the spec). I could only analyze the diff hunks provided. I could **not**: (a) read the rest of `scripts/verify-red-green.sh` beyond the shown hunk to see how `DEFAULT_TEST_GLOBS` is actually consumed downstream, (b) execute `hooks/tests/run.sh` / `hooks/tests/verify-red-green.test.sh`, or (c) inspect the shared test-harness helpers (`assert_eq`, `assert_contains`, `json_field`, `$SCRIPT`/`$GIT` setup) referenced but not shown. These are flagged explicitly rather than assumed clean.

### 🟠 Major (strongly recommended)

- `scripts/verify-red-green.sh:25-34` (new `DEFAULT_TEST_GLOBS`, per the added comment claiming "git `:(glob)` magic") — **The consuming code that actually matches these patterns against changed files is not present in this diff hunk and could not be located/verified in this review.** Git's `**` cross-directory glob semantics (leading `**/` matches zero-or-more dirs, trailing `/**` matches everything inside) are **glob pathspec-magic behavior** — they require the patterns to be passed through git with `:(glob)` magic (or `--glob-pathspecs`/`core.globPathSpecs`), not git's bare default pathspec matching. If downstream code instead matches these strings via plain bash `[[ $file == $pattern ]]` / `case` without `shopt -s globstar`, `**` silently collapses to `*` (no cross-`/` matching) and the fix is a no-op — the exact bug being "fixed" would persist. Impact: the entire fix's correctness hinges on this unverified detail. Fix direction: cite the exact call site (`grep -n DEFAULT_TEST_GLOBS scripts/verify-red-green.sh` for all usages) and confirm it is passed to `git diff`/`git ls-files` with glob pathspec magic; if it's bash-native matching, confirm `shopt -s globstar` is set and re-verify semantics match git's documented rules quoted in the comment (they differ subtly — e.g. bash globstar doesn't implement the "match in all directories including zero" rule for `**/foo` the same way).

- Test-suite execution not performed for this change (see session bound above). Per this repo's own policy (`hooks/tests/run.sh` must pass, non-zero → Critical), this is a **live-system fact** that cannot be certified via file:line alone. Mark UNVERIFIED. Fix direction: run `hooks/tests/run.sh` (or targeted `bash hooks/tests/verify-red-green.test.sh`) before merge; treat any failure as Critical per project policy, not as this review's Major.

### 🟡 Minor (recommended)

- `scripts/verify-red-green.sh:26-27,32-33` — Prefixing `*test*`/`*spec*` with `**/` correctly fixes the nested-path miss, but also **broadens the match surface to every depth**: any file whose path contains the substring "test" or "spec" anywhere (e.g. `docs/spec-notes.md`, `src/testing-utils.ts`, `vendor/pkg/test-runner.js`) now counts as a "test file changed" signal at any depth, whereas previously this overbroad substring match was at least confined to root-level basenames. This is an amplification of a pre-existing weak-pattern design, not a new class of bug, but it changes VALIDATED/INCONCLUSIVE classification behavior more broadly than the stated regression (nested test detection). Fix direction: consider tightening to extension-anchored patterns (`**/*.test.*`, `**/*_test.*`, `**/test_*.sh`) rather than bare substring matches, or explicitly accept the trade-off in the comment.

### 🔵 Suggestion (consider)

- `hooks/tests/verify-red-green.test.sh:66-91` — The new `test_validated_nested` asserts the fixed behavior but there's no accompanying mutation check (e.g., temporarily reverting the `**/` prefixes and confirming this exact test fails) demonstrating the test would have caught the regression pre-fix. Given this tool's own purpose is red/green validation, applying that same discipline to its own regression test would strengthen confidence the test isn't vacuously green. Fix direction: as a one-time local check before merge, revert `scripts/verify-red-green.sh`'s glob array to the pre-fix patterns and confirm `test_validated_nested` fails, then re-apply the fix and confirm it passes.

